import Foundation

struct Lesson: Identifiable {
    let key: String
    let title: String
    let subtitle: String
    let text: String
    var id: String { key }
    var plate: String { key == "larder" ? "sh_2" : "ls_" + key }
}

enum Lessons {
    static let all: [Lesson] = LessonsA.list + LessonsB.list
}

enum LessonsA {
    static let list: [Lesson] = [
        Lesson(key: "frostdates", title: "Reading Frost Dates", subtitle: "Two dates, and everything counted from them",
               text: "A vegetable garden is not planned by the month. It is planned from two nights: the last night in spring when the temperature is likely to touch freezing, and the first night in autumn when it does so again. Everything else is a count of weeks from one or the other. Peas go in six weeks before the last frost. Tomatoes go out one to two weeks after it. Garlic goes in six weeks before the first. Once the two dates are set, the whole year falls into place.\n\nThe dates are averages. A last frost date of April 1 means that in about half of the years there will be no frost after that night, and in the other half there will be one, usually a light one within the following fortnight. Nobody promises a warm April 2. That is why the hardy crops are sown weeks ahead of the date without worry, the half-hardy ones a little before it, the tender ones after it, and the very tender ones a week or two beyond that, when the soil as well as the air has warmed.\n\nThe usual way to find the dates is the hardiness zone, which is what this app does first. Zone 6 is April 1 and October 31; zone 4 is May 1 and October 1; zone 8 is March 1 and November 30. But a zone is a broad thing, and a garden is a small one. A slope that drains cold air downhill, a south wall that stores the day's heat, a low spot where the mist collects: each of these can move the true date by two weeks in either direction. Keep a note of the last frost you actually see each spring, and after three or four years you will know your own garden's number better than any map.\n\nThe frost-free season is the space between the two dates, and it is the second number to know. Zone 6 has about two hundred and ten days; zone 4 has one hundred and fifty; zone 3 has barely a hundred and twenty. A pumpkin that needs a hundred and ten days to ripen fits comfortably in the first, and only just in the third, and then only if it is started in a pot indoors. That is what the almanac's bars show: each crop's windows laid on your own frost-free season, so that a crop that does not fit is obvious before the seed is bought."),
        Lesson(key: "hardening", title: "Hardening Off", subtitle: "A week of days outside and nights in",
               text: "A seedling raised under a lamp or on a windowsill has grown in still air, even warmth and weak light. Its leaves are thin, its stem is soft, and the waxy coat that protects an outdoor leaf from sun and wind has not been built. Put it straight into the garden and it scorches, wilts and stalls for a fortnight, if it survives. Hardening off is the week that closes the gap.\n\nThe method is simple and the discipline is in keeping to it. On the first day the trays go out into a sheltered, shady spot for an hour or two and come back in. Each day the time grows and the shelter shrinks: a morning, then a whole day in dappled light, then a day in the sun, then a night outside if no frost is forecast. By the end of the week the plants have stayed out around the clock and the stems have thickened. A cold frame does the same job with less carrying: the lid is propped a little wider each day and left off at the end.\n\nWater less during the week, without letting the trays dry out, and do not feed. The point is to slow the soft growth and toughen what is there. Wind matters as much as light; a plant that has felt a breeze builds a stronger stem than one that has not, which is why a fan in the seedling room helps from the start.\n\nThe app schedules the job a week before each tray's planting-out window opens, counted from your last frost date. A tray of tomatoes for a zone 6 garden is started in mid-February, hardened off at the start of April and set out around the eighth, and the same arithmetic runs for peppers, brassicas, lettuce and everything else that begins indoors. Tick the job when the week is done and the transplant job follows on the day the window opens.\n\nOne exception: brassica transplants and lettuce, which are hardy or nearly so, can go out on a shorter schedule of three or four days. Tomatoes, peppers and eggplant, which resent any cold, want the full week and a soil temperature above fifty degrees before they leave the tray. Anything set out before its window, however well hardened, is likely to sit and sulk until the soil catches up with it."),
        Lesson(key: "sowing", title: "Direct or Indoors", subtitle: "The drill in the soil and the tray under the lamp",
               text: "Every crop is sown one of two ways, and the register tells you which. Direct sowing puts the seed where the plant will grow: a drill is drawn in the bed with the corner of a hoe or a finger, the seed is dropped along it at the crop's spacing, and the soil is drawn back over and firmed. Indoor sowing starts the seed in a tray or pot weeks earlier, under a lamp or on a windowsill, so that the plant has a head start before it goes out.\n\nSome crops must be direct sown. Carrots, parsnips, radishes and beets make a taproot that forks or stalls if it is disturbed, and they are cheap enough to sow thick and thin later. Peas and beans germinate fast and dislike having their roots handled. Corn and the cucurbits can be started in pots, but only in pots they can be tipped out of whole, and only a few weeks ahead. Others must be started inside because the season is not long enough for them from seed in the ground: tomato, pepper, eggplant, celery, leek and onion from seed all need six to twelve weeks of growing before the garden is warm enough for them.\n\nThe drill itself is a small craft. Depth is two to three times the seed's own size: a quarter inch for lettuce and carrot, an inch for peas, an inch and a half for beans, two inches for garlic cloves and fava beans. The soil is raked fine first so that small seed touches soil on all sides, and the drill is watered before sowing rather than after, which keeps the seed from being washed out of line. Seed dropped at the right spacing saves thinning later; seed dropped too thick makes a crowded row of thin plants that never bulk up.\n\nIn the plot, the drill is a gesture: press and hold a staked square, draw along the row, and seed drops at the crop's spacing as you go. Draw too far and the square is too dense, and a thinning job is scheduled for when the seedlings are up. Draw the right length and the spacing is right, which is one of the four things the larder grades at harvest.\n\nWhichever way a crop starts, the date is the same arithmetic: weeks before or after the last frost. Indoor sowings count back from the planting-out date; direct sowings count from the day the soil is fit. Cold, wet ground rots seed, so a direct sowing a week late into warm soil beats one on the calendar day into cold mud."),
        Lesson(key: "maturity", title: "Days to Maturity", subtitle: "The number on the packet, and where it starts",
               text: "Days to maturity is the most quoted and most misread number in a seed catalogue. It is the count of days from the plant going into its final bed to the first harvest under fair conditions. For a direct-sown crop that means from sowing. For a transplanted crop it means from the transplant, not from the seed, and the weeks under the lamp are extra. A tomato listed at seventy-five days that was started eight weeks earlier is really a hundred-and-thirty-day project.\n\nThe number is also a range in disguise. Cool weather slows everything; a cold June can add two weeks to a bean. Day length matters for some crops, onions above all, which bulb when the days reach a set length regardless of how long they have grown. So the register gives a range for each crop, and the plot counts from the lower figure to the first day the square can be pulled, and from the upper figure plus the harvest window to the last.\n\nIn practice the number is used backwards as often as forwards. A radish at twenty-five days can go in almost anywhere there is a gap. A parsnip at a hundred and twenty days needs the whole season and is sown once, early. A winter squash at a hundred and ten days needs to go in as soon as the soil is warm to ripen before the first frost, and in a short-season zone must be started in a pot to make the count. The app does this arithmetic for the succession crops: the last sowing it will offer is the one whose lower maturity figure still lands before your first frost date.\n\nThe harvest window follows maturity and is as important. Radish gives a week before it goes hollow; lettuce two weeks before it bolts; chard three months of picking. Pull inside the window and the larder grades the square well. Leave a crop past its window and it is marked spent, and the job list asks for the square to be cleared.\n\nWatch the plants as well as the calendar. A ripe tomato is heavy and gives slightly to the thumb; a cucumber is picked before the seeds harden; a melon slips from its stem; sweet corn is ready when the silks have browned and a pressed kernel spurts milk. The number gets you to the bed on the right week. The plant tells you the day."),
        Lesson(key: "spacing", title: "Square-Foot Spacing", subtitle: "One, four, nine or sixteen to the square",
               text: "The beds in this plot are drawn in square-foot cells because that is the simplest way to turn a spacing into a count. A crop that wants twelve inches between plants gets one to a square; six inches gives four, in a two-by-two grid; four inches gives nine; three inches gives sixteen. Peas and pole beans, which climb a support along one edge, are the exception at eight to a square in a double row.\n\nThe spacing is the room a plant needs for its roots and its leaves at full size, not at sowing. A cabbage seedling in a square looks lost for a month and then fills it to the edges. A carrot at three inches looks absurdly close until you remember the whole plant is a single root the width of a thumb. The mistake in almost every first garden is to crowd, and the result is a bed full of plants that are all half-size because none of them ever had enough.\n\nSowing to the count is easier than sowing to a ruler. For sixteen carrots, mark the square into four rows and drop four seeds along each; for nine beets, three by three; for four lettuces, the four corners a little in from the edge. Big seeds like beans and squash are placed one at a time. Small seeds are pinched and dropped, and the extras thinned when they have two true leaves, which is the moment they stop competing and start crowding.\n\nThinning is the part gardeners skip and regret. Pull the weakest seedlings, or snip them at the soil so that the roots of the ones left are not disturbed, and do it on a still evening so that the smell of bruised carrot leaf does not carry to the carrot fly. The thinnings of beet, lettuce and radish go straight into a salad.\n\nThe plot scores the sowing gesture against the count. Draw the drill for the right length and the square gets sixteen carrots; draw twice as far and it gets thirty, a thinning job is scheduled, and the spacing mark drops. That mark is one of the four that decide the grade at harvest, alongside the window, the rotation and the neighbours. A square sown to its number and thinned on time is a square that fills the crate at the end."),
        Lesson(key: "succession", title: "Succession Sowing", subtitle: "A short row every fortnight, not a long row once",
               text: "A packet of lettuce sown all at once gives forty heads in the same week, of which six are eaten and the rest bolt. The same packet sown a short row every two weeks from March to August gives a head or two every week for the whole season. That is succession sowing, and it is the difference between a garden that feeds a household and one that produces gluts and gaps.\n\nThe crops that suit it are the quick ones with a short harvest window: radish every ten days, lettuce and spinach and arugula every fortnight, bush beans and carrots and beets every three weeks. The register marks each with its interval, and the plot will offer the crop for sowing again at that interval until the last sowing that can ripen before the first frost. A sowing that would mature after that date is not offered; the arithmetic runs from your own frost dates.\n\nSuccession is also a matter of weather. Spinach and lettuce sown in May bolt in the June heat before they are worth cutting, so the sequence pauses in high summer and starts again in late July for autumn, when the cooling nights suit them. Radish does the same. Bush beans, which want the warmth, run the other way: their sequence starts after the last frost and ends about ten weeks before the first. Reading a crop's sequence against the year ruler in the almanac shows where the pauses fall.\n\nA second kind of succession fills a square after a crop is cleared. The peas that go in six weeks before the last frost are finished by early summer, and their square, richer in nitrogen for their having grown there, takes a sowing of beans or a late brassica for autumn. The early lettuce square takes a fall carrot. Planning the second crop at the same time as the first is what keeps a small plot full for the whole season.\n\nThe habit is to sow little and often and to keep a note. The app keeps the note for you: every sowing has its date on the square, its window on the ruler, and its next sowing suggested when the interval comes round. The gardener's job is to walk out with a short row of seed every other week, which is a good habit for other reasons too."),
        Lesson(key: "rotation", title: "Rotation Families", subtitle: "Move each family on, season by season",
               text: "Crops are grouped into families because relatives share their troubles. The cabbage family shares clubroot, a soil fungus that swells the roots and persists for seven years, and the cabbage white. The nightshades share blight and wilt. The alliums share white rot, which sits in the soil for fifteen years. Grow a family in the same bed two seasons running and whatever built up in the first season is waiting for the second. Move it on and the cycle is broken.\n\nThe rule in this plot is the simplest form of it: a bed that held a family last season should not hold that family this season. The plot checks it when you stake a crop and warns on a repeat; a family that has held a bed for two seasons already gets a strong warning on the third. The families are the ten in the almanac: brassica, allium, nightshade, cucurbit, legume, umbel, aster, amaranth, grass and the mint-family herbs, with the sundry perennials rotating freely because nothing else shares their diseases.\n\nThere is one pairing the plot rewards rather than warns on. Peas and beans carry rhizobia in their root nodules and leave the bed richer in nitrogen than they found it. The cabbages are the hungriest family for exactly that. So a bed that grew legumes last season and grows brassicas this season is marked as good rotation, and the square earns its grade at harvest for it.\n\nThe classic four-year cycle runs legumes, then brassicas, then roots and onions, then potatoes and tomatoes, and back to legumes. It does not need to be followed to the letter. A small plot with three beds runs a three-year cycle; a plot that grows no potatoes drops that year. What matters is that the family moves, and that the heavy feeders follow the legumes.\n\nThe record is kept per bed. When you close the season in autumn, every family that stood in each bed is written into that bed's history, and the next spring's staking is checked against it. The families this season are shown on every bed's page, so a repeat is visible before it is made. Two seasons of the same family in one bed is a mistake anyone can make once; the plot's job is to make it hard to make twice.")
    ]
}

enum LessonsB {
    static let list: [Lesson] = [
        Lesson(key: "companions", title: "Companion Planting", subtitle: "What is real, what is folklore",
               text: "Companion planting is the oldest advice in the garden and the least examined. Some of it is sound, some of it is habit dressed as wisdom, and it pays to know which is which before a bed is laid out around it.\n\nThe pairings with evidence behind them are mostly about insects. The flowering members of the daisy family, calendula, marigold and the like, and the umbels, dill, fennel and cilantro left to flower, draw hoverflies, lacewings and small parasitic wasps whose larvae eat aphids and caterpillars. A plot with a few of them in flower has fewer pests, and that is measurable. French marigold roots release a compound that reduces root-knot nematodes in the soil over a season, which is why they have been planted with tomatoes for as long as anyone remembers. Nasturtium is a trap crop: aphids and cabbage white caterpillars prefer it, and they go there instead of the cabbages.\n\nThe pairings about growth are mostly about space and light. The three sisters, corn, pole beans and squash, work because the corn gives the beans a pole, the beans feed the soil, and the squash shades out weeds; it is a spacing arrangement more than a chemistry. Lettuce under tomatoes works because the lettuce gets shade in July. Radish with carrot works because the radish is up in five days and marks the row.\n\nThe antagonists are fewer and better founded. Fennel is allelopathic and stunts most neighbours, so it stands alone. Sunflowers do the same to potatoes and pole beans. Alliums slow peas and beans. Strawberries and brassicas both do worse near each other, and tomatoes and potatoes share blight, so the plot warns on both.\n\nMuch of the rest, that basil improves the flavour of tomatoes, that carrots love tomatoes, that sage keeps every pest away, is pleasant and unproven. It does no harm, which is more than can be said of a lot of advice. In this plot a companion beside a square lifts the grade at harvest by a step and an antagonist beside it drops the grade by one, using the register's lists. The lists lean on the pairings with evidence and treat the rest as neutral."),
        Lesson(key: "mulchwater", title: "Mulch and Water", subtitle: "Deep at the root, and a cover to keep it there",
               text: "Most of the trouble in a summer bed comes from water that arrives in fits and starts. Blossom-end rot on tomatoes, split cabbages, bitter cucumbers, bolting lettuce, woody radishes: each of them is a plant that was dry and then suddenly wet, or the reverse. The cure is not more water but steadier water, and the two tools for that are a good watering habit and a mulch.\n\nWater deeply and less often. A sprinkle every evening wets the top inch, dries by noon, and teaches the roots to stay near the surface where they suffer first. A slow soak once or twice a week, enough to wet the soil a hand deep, sends the roots down where the moisture holds. Water at the root, not over the leaves: wet leaves in the evening are an invitation to mildew and blight. The morning is the best time; the plant has the day to use it and the leaves dry quickly.\n\nA mulch is anything laid over the soil between the plants: straw, dried grass clippings, shredded leaves, compost. It stops the surface drying, evens the soil temperature, smothers the small weeds, and keeps rain from splashing soil, and the spores in it, up onto the lower leaves. Lay it two or three inches deep once the soil has warmed in late spring; laid too early it keeps the ground cold and slows the tender crops. Keep it an inch back from the stems so that slugs have no bridge.\n\nSome crops want more than others. The cucurbits and the brassicas drink hard; the alliums and the herbs of the mint family, sage, thyme, rosemary and oregano, would rather be dry than wet and rot in a damp bed. Beans and peas want water while they flower and set. Carrots want it steady from sowing to the last thinning and then a little less, or the roots split.\n\nThe plot puts a watering job on a bed whenever something in it is growing, the day is not a rain day, and the bed has not been watered. Drag the can across the squares or tick the job; either way the record shows the bed was watered that day. It is a small habit with a large effect, and the rain in the corner of the scene tells you the days you can skip it."),
        Lesson(key: "fallcrops", title: "Fall and Overwinter", subtitle: "The second season, counted back from the first frost",
               text: "The spring sowing is the one everybody makes. The autumn sowing is the one that separates a garden that feeds a household through October from one that is bare by September. It is planned the same way, but the count runs backwards from the first fall frost instead of forwards from the last spring one.\n\nThe method is to take a crop's days to maturity, add a fortnight because the days are shortening and growth slows, and count that far back from the first frost date. A fifty-day spinach for a garden with an October 31 frost is sown in the first week of September; a sixty-day carrot goes in by mid-August; a ninety-day cabbage is transplanted in late July, which means its tray was sown in June. The register carries a fall window for every crop that has one, and the almanac draws it on the ruler beside the spring bars.\n\nThe cool-season crops are the ones that thrive in it. Lettuce, spinach, arugula, mizuna, radish and the Asian greens that bolt in June are sweeter and cleaner in October. Kale, leeks, Brussels sprouts, parsnips and the late cabbages stand into real cold and improve for it; a frost turns their starch to sugar. Peas sown in August give a small crop in October. Garlic is the one that goes in as everything else comes out, four to eight weeks before the first frost, to root before winter and lift the following July.\n\nProtection extends the season. A cloche or a low tunnel of fleece over a bed of spinach or lettuce holds off the first light frosts and keeps the leaves clean; a cold frame over a sowing of winter salad gives cuttings until Christmas in most zones. Fleece over the tender crops on a night of forecast frost, and taken off again in the morning, can buy the tomatoes another fortnight.\n\nThe plot's weather is drawn from the date, and on the days it shows frost within a fortnight of your frost dates the job list asks you to cover the tender squares. Tick it and the record shows the beds were covered. After the first frost date has passed, the job to close the season appears, and the families in each bed are written into its history for next year's rotation."),
        Lesson(key: "seedsaving", title: "Seed Saving", subtitle: "Dry, label, and keep the true ones",
               text: "Saving seed was the whole of gardening once, and it is still the cheapest way to have next year's crop and the only way to have a variety that suits your own ground. The rules are few, but they matter, and the first is to know which crops come true.\n\nSelf-pollinating annuals are the easy ones. Tomato, pepper, bean, pea and lettuce mostly pollinate themselves before the flower opens, so the seed makes a plant like its parent even with other varieties nearby. Save from the best plant, not the first fruit; the earliest tomato on a poor plant passes on the poor plant. Let the fruit ripen fully, past eating, on the plant. Tomato seed is squeezed into a jar with a little water and left three days to ferment, which strips the gel that stops it sprouting, then rinsed and dried on a plate. Beans and peas are left in the pod until it rattles.\n\nThe cross-pollinators are the hard ones. Squash, pumpkin, cucumber and melon cross with everything of their kind within a bee's flight, and the seed from a pumpkin grown beside a zucchini grows something that is neither. Corn crosses on the wind. Brassicas cross with each other. Seed from these comes true only with a quarter mile of isolation or hand pollination with the flower bagged, and it is usually better bought.\n\nThe biennials, carrot, beet, onion, parsnip, cabbage, make seed in their second year, which means holding the root through winter and replanting it in spring for a tall stalk of flower in June. It is worth doing once, for the education, and then it is worth doing only for a variety you cannot buy.\n\nHybrids, marked F1 on the packet, are a cross of two parent lines and do not come true; the seed grows a scatter of the grandparents. Save only from open-pollinated kinds.\n\nDry everything thoroughly, out of the sun, until a seed snaps rather than bends. Store in paper envelopes inside a jar with a sachet of dried rice, somewhere cool and dark. Label every envelope with the variety and the year; nothing looks more alike than two kinds of bean seed in March. Most seed keeps three to five years. Parsnip and onion keep one, and are bought fresh."),
        Lesson(key: "covercrops", title: "Cover Crops", subtitle: "Green manure that holds the soil through winter",
               text: "A bed left bare through the winter loses its soil to rain and its nitrogen to leaching, and grows a fine crop of weeds by March. A bed sown with a cover crop in September holds its soil, shades out the weeds, keeps the worms working, and is turned in as green manure a month before the spring sowing. It is the cheapest improvement a plot can be given, and the one most often skipped because nothing is eaten from it.\n\nThe usual cover crops are the cereals and the legumes. Winter rye is sown thickly in early autumn, stands green through the cold, and makes a mass of root that is dug in before it goes to seed in spring. Vetch and crimson clover are legumes that fix nitrogen as the peas do and leave it in the bed. Field peas do the same and are cut earlier. Mustard is quick and cheap but is a brassica, and a bed that grows it counts as having grown a brassica for the rotation, which the plan must remember. Phacelia is none of the families in the almanac and can go anywhere.\n\nThe timing is counted from the first frost like everything else in autumn. The cover needs four to six weeks of growth before hard frost to establish, so it is sown as the summer beds are cleared, from late August to early October depending on the zone. A bed that is harvested late, a bed of leeks or Brussels sprouts, is simply left with its crop and is not covered.\n\nIn spring the cover is cut down and dug in, or cut and left on the surface as a mulch, three or four weeks before the bed is wanted. Green material dug in needs those weeks to rot before seed is sown into it; sown too soon the seedlings starve while the microbes take the nitrogen to break down the rye. Rye in particular should be turned before it flowers, when the stems are still soft.\n\nIn the plot, a bed with nothing growing after the first frost is shown under cover crop through the winter on the season slider, and the beds are cleared and their families recorded when the season is closed. The almanac's year ruler shows where the autumn window for a cover falls against your frost dates. A bed under rye in December is not idle; it is being made ready."),
        Lesson(key: "larder", title: "Reading the Larder", subtitle: "What the grades mean and how a slot improves",
               text: "Every square pulled from the plot goes to the larder, one slot per crop, and every pull is graded. The grade is not a score for effort. It is a record of four things the plan can see: whether the square was pulled inside its harvest window, whether it was grown against the bed's rotation, whether it stood beside a companion or an antagonist, and whether it was sown at its spacing.\n\nInside the window counts most. A crop pulled in the days between its lower maturity figure and the end of its window is at its best; pulled before, it is small, and pulled after, it is woody, hollow, bolted or split, depending on the crop. Rotation counts next: a square whose family did not stand in that bed last season, or that followed a legume with a brassica, earns its mark. A companion beside the square adds a step and an antagonist beside it takes one away. Spacing is the mark the drill gesture set at sowing, lifted if the square was thinned on time.\n\nFour marks or more is Prize; two or three is Good; below that is Fair. The larder keeps the best grade a crop has reached and counts every pull beneath it, so a Fair slot from a hurried first season is replaced the day a Good pull of the same crop comes in. Tap any slot to see the plate, the date of the best pull, the bed it came from and the season number.\n\nThe seasons page is the other half of the record. When the season is closed after the first frost, the plot writes a page for it: how many squares were sown, how many pulls came in, how many at Prize, which families stood in the beds, and which year it was. The pages are how a plan learns. A season with many Fair grades and few Prize is usually a season of late pulls, and the fix is to open the plot more often in the harvest weeks.\n\nNothing in the larder is locked. Every crop has a slot from the first day, empty until it is filled, and the plates on the empty slots are drawn faintly so that the shelves show what is still to grow. Twenty different crops on the shelves earns the Full Shelves badge; a pull in each of the four seasons earns the Four-Season Plot.")
    ]
}
